1
00:00:12,273 --> 00:00:14,362
[seagulls crying,
waves crashing]

2
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:20,542
Poor girl.

3
00:00:20,629 --> 00:00:21,891
Yeah.

4
00:00:21,978 --> 00:00:23,893
Bruising and abrasions
on the neck suggest

5
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:25,590
some sort
of physical attack.

6
00:00:25,677 --> 00:00:27,244
That's why
they called us in.

7
00:00:27,331 --> 00:00:30,073
- No clothes, no I.D.?
- Nothing.

8
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:32,597
Doesn't look like she was
in the water for long.

9
00:00:32,684 --> 00:00:34,643
Contact the coast guards,
see if they've had

10
00:00:34,730 --> 00:00:36,906
any distress calls
or missing person reports.

11
00:00:36,993 --> 00:00:39,778
Yeah.

12
00:00:39,865 --> 00:00:41,911
There's the guy
who found her.

13
00:00:41,998 --> 00:00:44,827
William Gardiner.

14
00:00:44,914 --> 00:00:47,177
He saw the body
from up on the bluff.

15
00:00:47,264 --> 00:00:49,484
- 'Mr. Gardiner?'
- Thank you, sir.

16
00:00:49,571 --> 00:00:51,094
I'm Agent Lisbon.

17
00:00:51,181 --> 00:00:52,617
You wanna tell me
what happened?

18
00:00:52,704 --> 00:00:55,272
I was coming
to check on the monarchs.

19
00:00:55,359 --> 00:00:57,535
Butterflies.
He runs a sanctuary.

20
00:00:57,622 --> 00:00:59,842
I was up there.

21
00:00:59,929 --> 00:01:02,323
Where he's standing,
the man in the suit.

22
00:01:11,245 --> 00:01:12,202
[male #1]
Hey!

23
00:01:12,289 --> 00:01:13,334
[whistles]

24
00:01:21,385 --> 00:01:23,822
- You with the police?
- 'Yes, I am.'

25
00:01:23,909 --> 00:01:25,476
[snorts]

26
00:01:28,523 --> 00:01:29,480
Patrick Jane.

27
00:01:29,567 --> 00:01:31,091
Jed Stack.

28
00:01:31,178 --> 00:01:33,745
This is my land.

29
00:01:34,746 --> 00:01:36,313
Hey, Jed.

30
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:40,491
There's a dead woman
on the beach in Wracker's Cove.

31
00:01:40,578 --> 00:01:41,666
Yeah.

32
00:01:41,753 --> 00:01:43,320
Well, who is she?

33
00:01:43,407 --> 00:01:45,453
Don't know.

34
00:01:45,540 --> 00:01:46,628
Any idea?

35
00:01:46,715 --> 00:01:48,499
No.

36
00:01:48,586 --> 00:01:49,587
How would I know?

37
00:01:49,674 --> 00:01:52,068
Eh, your land.

38
00:01:52,155 --> 00:01:55,854
I thought
maybe, uh, your body.

39
00:01:57,726 --> 00:01:59,249
That's absurd.

40
00:01:59,336 --> 00:02:01,730
Sorry. I'm just pokin' at ya.
It's often instructive.

41
00:02:01,817 --> 00:02:05,299
Why don't you come meet
my colleagues?

42
00:02:05,386 --> 00:02:07,605
[horse blusters]

43
00:02:12,132 --> 00:02:14,003
This is Jed Stack.

44
00:02:14,090 --> 00:02:15,961
Agent Teresa Lisbon.

45
00:02:16,048 --> 00:02:18,312
Agent Grace Van Pelt.
This is Jed's land.

46
00:02:18,399 --> 00:02:21,141
How do you do, ma'am?
Ma'am.

47
00:02:21,228 --> 00:02:24,013
Mr. Gardiner,
what are you doing here?

48
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:25,493
I found her.

49
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:27,495
I was checking
on the monarchs.

50
00:02:27,582 --> 00:02:29,018
Butterflies.

51
00:02:29,105 --> 00:02:30,672
Yes. I saw some.

52
00:02:30,759 --> 00:02:32,500
You were trespassing.

53
00:02:32,587 --> 00:02:35,024
For God sakes, forget about
your property rights for once.

54
00:02:35,111 --> 00:02:36,765
A woman's dead.

55
00:02:36,852 --> 00:02:39,681
The woman's death has nothing
to do with your trespassing.

56
00:02:39,768 --> 00:02:41,596
So she's on your land?

57
00:02:41,683 --> 00:02:45,426
No. Strictly speaking, my land
ends at the high-water mark.

58
00:02:45,513 --> 00:02:47,297
She's on state land.

59
00:02:47,384 --> 00:02:48,690
But given the location

60
00:02:48,777 --> 00:02:52,041
perhaps she's a tenant
or an employee of yours?

61
00:02:52,128 --> 00:02:54,522
I have no tenants,
and I employ no women.

62
00:02:54,609 --> 00:02:57,220
And besides, she was obviously
washed up by the sea.

63
00:02:57,307 --> 00:02:58,961
She must've drifted over
from the mainland

64
00:02:59,048 --> 00:03:00,484
or fell off a ship.

65
00:03:00,571 --> 00:03:03,357
Nothing to do with me
or my ranch or this island.

66
00:03:03,444 --> 00:03:05,620
It seems to make sense.

67
00:03:05,707 --> 00:03:09,101
Nice to meet you, Mr. Stack,
and, uh, butterfly man.

68
00:03:09,189 --> 00:03:10,755
- Gardiner, yeah?
- Yes.

69
00:03:10,842 --> 00:03:12,757
Neither of you were
born here, were you?

70
00:03:12,844 --> 00:03:14,019
No.

71
00:03:14,106 --> 00:03:15,804
What's that got to do
with anything?

72
00:03:15,891 --> 00:03:18,546
Nothing. Just lucky to live
in such a beautiful place.

73
00:03:22,202 --> 00:03:24,595
So, Mr. Stack..

74
00:03:38,130 --> 00:03:40,045
[instrumental music]

75
00:03:41,003 --> 00:03:42,178
[grunts]

76
00:03:53,711 --> 00:03:56,018
Good idea.

77
00:03:56,105 --> 00:03:57,846
We'll see.

78
00:04:03,068 --> 00:04:04,418
Beautiful, isn't it?

79
00:04:04,505 --> 00:04:06,376
For a crime scene. Yeah.

80
00:04:09,118 --> 00:04:12,034
[theme music]

81
00:04:17,909 --> 00:04:19,389
[Cho]
Dental records
just came in.

82
00:04:19,476 --> 00:04:22,262
The victim's Talia Suarez,
19 years old.

83
00:04:22,349 --> 00:04:24,133
Raised in
the foster care system.

84
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:27,310
Check with family services. Find
out what they know about her.

85
00:04:27,397 --> 00:04:29,443
Rigsby and Van Pelt
are on their way.

86
00:04:29,530 --> 00:04:31,619
Is there anything on
the coast guard front?

87
00:04:31,706 --> 00:04:33,664
They've had no reports
of anyone missing overboard.

88
00:04:33,751 --> 00:04:35,753
What about the mainland
across from the island?

89
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:38,190
We've got local PD canvassing
all the marinas,

90
00:04:38,278 --> 00:04:40,758
hotels, and motels along that
stretch of coast. Nothing yet.

91
00:04:40,845 --> 00:04:42,760
- Alright. Keep me posted.
- Where are you going?

92
00:04:42,847 --> 00:04:44,588
Back to the island.

93
00:04:44,675 --> 00:04:47,069
The island? Why? If her body
came from the mainland--

94
00:04:47,156 --> 00:04:48,679
Jane's got a theory.

95
00:04:48,766 --> 00:04:49,680
Right.

96
00:04:56,252 --> 00:04:57,209
Ah.

97
00:05:01,649 --> 00:05:03,477
Nice. It's not
what we're looking for.

98
00:05:03,564 --> 00:05:05,479
You could make
a necklace out of it.

99
00:05:05,566 --> 00:05:07,394
I could.

100
00:05:07,481 --> 00:05:11,354
You should. You should have
some kind of restful hobby.

101
00:05:14,923 --> 00:05:16,838
I don't see it.

102
00:05:16,925 --> 00:05:20,189
Hard to believe the moon
causes tides, isn't it?

103
00:05:20,276 --> 00:05:23,758
I mean, we all know it does,
but it just seems so unlikely.

104
00:05:23,845 --> 00:05:26,717
I guess. I've never really
thought about it.

105
00:05:26,804 --> 00:05:28,589
That's 'cause you're
a city girl.

106
00:05:28,676 --> 00:05:29,938
A-ha!

107
00:05:31,026 --> 00:05:33,855
My theory made concrete.

108
00:05:33,942 --> 00:05:34,856
I told you.

109
00:05:34,943 --> 00:05:36,118
Yes, you did.

110
00:05:38,294 --> 00:05:40,514
I thought I saw
a tea shop in town.

111
00:05:40,601 --> 00:05:43,038
I could use a cup of tea.
What about you?

112
00:05:43,125 --> 00:05:44,735
You need a hand with that?

113
00:05:44,822 --> 00:05:46,694
[Patrick]
'I can manage.'

114
00:05:59,315 --> 00:06:01,578
[exhales deeply]

115
00:06:07,367 --> 00:06:08,890
[rattles doorknob]
Uh.

116
00:06:10,108 --> 00:06:12,894
Uh. Well, that's disappointing.

117
00:06:12,981 --> 00:06:14,722
It's closed.

118
00:06:14,809 --> 00:06:17,028
Picturesque, isn't it?

119
00:06:17,115 --> 00:06:20,205
In a desolate sort of way.
Where are all the locals?

120
00:06:20,292 --> 00:06:22,338
Good question.

121
00:06:22,425 --> 00:06:24,209
[cell phone rings]

122
00:06:24,296 --> 00:06:25,776
Cho.

123
00:06:25,863 --> 00:06:27,778
Coroner came back
with a preliminary report.

124
00:06:27,865 --> 00:06:30,085
Larynx was crushed
by a blow or heavy pressure.

125
00:06:30,172 --> 00:06:31,565
'No water in the lungs.'

126
00:06:31,652 --> 00:06:32,696
So she didn't drown.

127
00:06:32,783 --> 00:06:34,176
Dead before she went in.

128
00:06:34,263 --> 00:06:35,786
Also, physical evidence
indicates

129
00:06:35,873 --> 00:06:37,788
she was sexually assaulted
before she was killed.

130
00:06:37,875 --> 00:06:39,311
'Was there any DNA recovered?'

131
00:06:39,399 --> 00:06:41,096
'Yeah, but no matches
in the database.'

132
00:06:41,183 --> 00:06:43,664
Check out the background
of the men on the island.

133
00:06:43,751 --> 00:06:46,797
'See if any of them have
a history of sexual assault.'

134
00:06:46,884 --> 00:06:49,713
See you soon, I hope.

135
00:06:52,499 --> 00:06:55,763
Just like the tea shop.
There's nobody here.

136
00:06:55,850 --> 00:06:58,983
- Where is everybody?
- It's intriguing.

137
00:06:59,070 --> 00:07:01,072
A mass vanishment.

138
00:07:01,159 --> 00:07:02,726
That's not even a word.

139
00:07:02,813 --> 00:07:04,641
Well, it's been known
to happen.

140
00:07:04,728 --> 00:07:07,601
Roanoke colony. The Olmecs.

141
00:07:07,688 --> 00:07:09,516
[Lisbon]
Hello? Hello?

142
00:07:09,603 --> 00:07:11,300
I know where they are,
Lisbon.

143
00:07:11,387 --> 00:07:14,825
I can picture them gathered
together in a dimly lit room

144
00:07:14,912 --> 00:07:17,611
plotting their mysterious
island purpose.

145
00:07:17,698 --> 00:07:19,743
That's really creepy.

146
00:07:19,830 --> 00:07:21,876
Relax.

147
00:07:25,183 --> 00:07:27,838
Talia Suarez. Mm.

148
00:07:27,925 --> 00:07:30,101
Yes, uh, we were hoping
you could tell us--

149
00:07:30,188 --> 00:07:31,668
Murdered, huh?

150
00:07:31,755 --> 00:07:33,670
That's too bad.

151
00:07:33,757 --> 00:07:37,587
But then, deadbeat kids like her
nearly always end up badly.

152
00:07:37,674 --> 00:07:39,459
"Deadbeat kids like her"?

153
00:07:39,546 --> 00:07:43,332
Oh, we see it all the time.
She was sent to us at 6.

154
00:07:43,419 --> 00:07:46,422
Mother died of a drug overdose.
Father murdered.

155
00:07:46,509 --> 00:07:48,555
Oh, and a problem
from the first.

156
00:07:48,642 --> 00:07:51,993
Insubordinate, argumentative,
resistant to authority.

157
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:54,256
It's a long list.

158
00:07:54,343 --> 00:07:56,040
Pretty girl, looked like.

159
00:07:56,127 --> 00:08:00,088
Vivacious, but no discipline.
No common sense.

160
00:08:00,175 --> 00:08:02,743
What about her foster parents?
Can we talk to them?

161
00:08:02,830 --> 00:08:04,396
Didn't have any.

162
00:08:04,484 --> 00:08:06,921
Kept running away from
the homes we placed her in.

163
00:08:07,008 --> 00:08:09,489
Ended up finishing her time
in a group home.

164
00:08:11,099 --> 00:08:12,927
Can we get that number?

165
00:08:13,014 --> 00:08:16,278
Maybe there are friends
we can talk to.

166
00:08:16,365 --> 00:08:19,368
I'll, uh, see what I can do
and get back to you.

167
00:08:19,455 --> 00:08:23,154
You know, the truth is,
some of them..

168
00:08:23,241 --> 00:08:25,461
[lowered voice]
...you just can't help.

169
00:08:25,548 --> 00:08:26,897
[clicks]

170
00:08:26,984 --> 00:08:28,595
[dials]

171
00:08:30,248 --> 00:08:32,947
Thanks for your time.

172
00:08:33,034 --> 00:08:34,339
Hey.

173
00:08:36,907 --> 00:08:39,083
You know we're never gonna
hear from her again.

174
00:08:39,170 --> 00:08:41,390
Not since you knocked
her coffee over her desk.

175
00:08:41,477 --> 00:08:43,435
She wasn't gonna
do anything to help us.

176
00:08:43,523 --> 00:08:45,742
It's a tough job.
Maybe she's doing her best.

177
00:08:45,829 --> 00:08:49,050
She puts in her 20 so she
gets her pension. Pisses me off.

178
00:08:49,137 --> 00:08:50,791
- I noticed.
- I do my job.

179
00:08:50,878 --> 00:08:53,315
You don't hear me complain.
Why can't others do theirs

180
00:08:53,402 --> 00:08:55,360
so maybe a kid
doesn't have to die.

181
00:08:55,447 --> 00:08:56,884
Alright. I'll drive.
Gimme the keys.

182
00:08:56,971 --> 00:08:58,233
That's alright.
I wanna drive.

183
00:08:58,320 --> 00:09:00,278
I don't think so.
Give me the keys.

184
00:09:00,365 --> 00:09:01,802
- What's with you?
- You're worked up.

185
00:09:01,889 --> 00:09:03,107
I should drive,
so gimme--

186
00:09:03,194 --> 00:09:04,935
- Cut it out..
- Give me the keys.

187
00:09:05,022 --> 00:09:06,981
- Come on. Just--
- What's the matter with you?

188
00:09:09,157 --> 00:09:11,638
Okay. No problem. You drive.

189
00:09:20,298 --> 00:09:24,912
Now two additional
ferry crossings a weekend

190
00:09:24,999 --> 00:09:30,395
during the summer months
equals a 35% increase

191
00:09:30,482 --> 00:09:32,528
in revenues projected.

192
00:09:32,615 --> 00:09:35,052
Now, I don't understand
how anyone can be against that.

193
00:09:35,139 --> 00:09:36,706
The runoff from Stack's
cattle ranch

194
00:09:36,793 --> 00:09:39,230
is already affecting
the groundwater pH balance.

195
00:09:39,317 --> 00:09:41,276
- Another thing that you--
- Point of order, chair.

196
00:09:41,363 --> 00:09:43,104
- I had the floor.
- Oh, now, Peter.

197
00:09:43,191 --> 00:09:46,150
- Let's not be so fussy.
- Excuse me for interrupting.

198
00:09:46,237 --> 00:09:48,500
I'm agent Teresa Lisbon
from the CBI.

199
00:09:48,588 --> 00:09:51,068
And this is my colleague,
Patrick Jane.

200
00:09:51,155 --> 00:09:53,593
I'm sorry, but we're
in the middle of a meeting.

201
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:55,595
- Can you come back later?
- No, we can't.

202
00:09:55,682 --> 00:09:56,900
A woman was murdered here.

203
00:09:56,987 --> 00:09:59,207
We're here to discover
who did it and why.

204
00:09:59,294 --> 00:10:01,688
Her name was Talia Suarez.

205
00:10:01,775 --> 00:10:03,733
Can any of you
remember seeing her?

206
00:10:03,820 --> 00:10:05,082
[Peter]
- 'Sir.'
- Mm?

207
00:10:05,169 --> 00:10:07,432
- Wasn't she found on the beach?
- Yes.

208
00:10:07,519 --> 00:10:09,260
Well, I mean,
that would indicate

209
00:10:09,347 --> 00:10:11,785
that the tide, uh,
brought her in, surely.

210
00:10:11,872 --> 00:10:13,569
That's mainland business.

211
00:10:13,656 --> 00:10:16,485
Yes, we have nothing to do
with happens on the mainland.

212
00:10:16,572 --> 00:10:19,053
Messy place, the mainland.
That's why you're all here.

213
00:10:19,140 --> 00:10:21,838
To escape from all that,
and of course, she's from there

214
00:10:21,925 --> 00:10:23,623
because no one here
knows her, right?

215
00:10:23,710 --> 00:10:24,841
That's right.

216
00:10:24,928 --> 00:10:26,974
There's a lot of flotsam
that washes up

217
00:10:27,061 --> 00:10:29,019
on San Felix all the time,
doesn't it?

218
00:10:29,106 --> 00:10:30,717
- Certainly.
- Mm-hmm.

219
00:10:30,804 --> 00:10:33,763
Summer weekends, the harbor
gets all sorts of trash.

220
00:10:33,850 --> 00:10:35,896
[sighs]
Especially after storms, yes.

221
00:10:35,983 --> 00:10:37,985
Excuse me. If you would?
Thank you.

222
00:10:38,072 --> 00:10:41,684
So here's your island. It's a
beautiful island, I must say.

223
00:10:41,771 --> 00:10:45,122
Here's your harbor, on the
leeward side of the island

224
00:10:45,209 --> 00:10:47,821
closest to the mainland coast.

225
00:10:47,908 --> 00:10:50,954
And the body was found
in Wracker's Cove

226
00:10:51,041 --> 00:10:54,001
which is here, I believe.

227
00:10:54,088 --> 00:10:57,047
On the western side of
the island.

228
00:10:57,134 --> 00:11:00,529
The furthest side
from the mainland coast.

229
00:11:00,616 --> 00:11:02,662
Now, I would think
it would be difficult

230
00:11:02,749 --> 00:11:05,534
for a body to drift from here
against the currents

231
00:11:05,621 --> 00:11:08,058
and the prevailing winds,
to here.

232
00:11:08,145 --> 00:11:09,843
Another compelling fact.

233
00:11:09,930 --> 00:11:12,715
If you throw something
into the water in Wracker's Cove

234
00:11:12,802 --> 00:11:15,413
it will drift out
to sea for a bit.

235
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:18,721
And then, it will come
back to shore.

236
00:11:18,808 --> 00:11:21,071
I checked with this.

237
00:11:23,073 --> 00:11:26,424
Talia Suarez died here.

238
00:11:26,511 --> 00:11:28,209
So look to your neighbor
and think

239
00:11:28,296 --> 00:11:30,733
'how well do you know them?'

240
00:11:30,820 --> 00:11:34,998
Because someone on this island
committed murder.

241
00:11:35,085 --> 00:11:36,260
[crowd murmuring]

242
00:11:36,347 --> 00:11:37,784
We'll be back.

243
00:11:39,568 --> 00:11:42,484
[crowd murmuring]

244
00:11:50,187 --> 00:11:52,233
I've been doing my best to track
Talia after leaving foster care.

245
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:54,539
- And? Anything useful?
- A series of minimum wage jobs.

246
00:11:54,626 --> 00:11:57,107
She never stayed longer
than six weeks at any one.

247
00:11:57,194 --> 00:11:59,109
- Moved all over the state.
- What else? Friends?

248
00:11:59,196 --> 00:12:00,894
- Acquaintances?
- Nothing yet.

249
00:12:00,981 --> 00:12:03,679
I talked to employers.
They say she was a good worker.

250
00:12:03,766 --> 00:12:06,247
- Kept to herself.
- What about family services?

251
00:12:06,334 --> 00:12:08,075
They're no help either.
It's sad, boss.

252
00:12:08,162 --> 00:12:10,120
We're the only ones
that care about her.

253
00:12:10,207 --> 00:12:12,035
Keep looking.

254
00:12:12,122 --> 00:12:14,821
There's footage of her getting
on the ferry to San Felix.

255
00:12:14,908 --> 00:12:17,127
- No camera on the island side.
- Uh, what's this?

256
00:12:17,214 --> 00:12:18,868
Police sent it over.
Talia's room.

257
00:12:18,955 --> 00:12:20,217
Beachfront motel
on the coast

258
00:12:20,304 --> 00:12:22,350
'across the strait
from San Felix.'

259
00:12:22,437 --> 00:12:24,221
Toothbrush, toothpaste,
a change of clothes?

260
00:12:24,308 --> 00:12:25,657
That's it?

261
00:12:25,745 --> 00:12:27,834
- That and a notebook.
- What notebook?

262
00:12:27,921 --> 00:12:28,965
Jane's got it.

263
00:12:32,099 --> 00:12:35,058
It's a diary of sorts. She wrote
letters to her dead parents.

264
00:12:35,145 --> 00:12:36,712
That's sad.

265
00:12:36,799 --> 00:12:39,497
Then there's this, I found
tucked away in the back pages.

266
00:12:39,584 --> 00:12:41,412
"San Felix fish." Mm.

267
00:12:47,984 --> 00:12:49,681
Does this seem
too easy to you?

268
00:12:49,769 --> 00:12:52,772
Only one way to find out.

269
00:13:01,258 --> 00:13:02,825
Who's in charge here?

270
00:13:02,912 --> 00:13:04,348
We ain't open to tourists.

271
00:13:04,435 --> 00:13:07,003
[sniffs]
Crab.

272
00:13:07,090 --> 00:13:09,179
You can't be out here
for safety reasons.

273
00:13:09,266 --> 00:13:11,703
We're with the California Bureau
of Investigation.

274
00:13:11,791 --> 00:13:13,967
We need to ask you some
questions, mister...

275
00:13:14,054 --> 00:13:16,796
Lafleur. Jack Lafleur.

276
00:13:16,883 --> 00:13:18,536
So what's this all about?

277
00:13:18,623 --> 00:13:22,192
I don't think you're gonna want
to talk to us out here, jack.

278
00:13:22,279 --> 00:13:24,760
Uh, is there somewhere
a little more private?

279
00:13:26,457 --> 00:13:29,112
Get back to work.

280
00:13:29,199 --> 00:13:31,071
Let's go to my office.

281
00:13:37,991 --> 00:13:40,820
So what's this all about?

282
00:13:40,907 --> 00:13:43,692
Tell me about Talia Suarez.

283
00:13:43,779 --> 00:13:46,608
What? Who's Talia Suarez?

284
00:13:46,695 --> 00:13:48,740
The girl we found murdered
in Wracker's Cove.

285
00:13:48,828 --> 00:13:50,568
What's that got to do
with me?

286
00:13:50,655 --> 00:13:52,657
We found this in her diary.

287
00:13:54,616 --> 00:13:56,183
That doesn't make any sense.

288
00:13:56,270 --> 00:13:58,359
Ah, there we are.

289
00:13:58,446 --> 00:14:00,491
Hey, hey. Those are mine.

290
00:14:00,578 --> 00:14:03,016
[papers rustling]

291
00:14:03,103 --> 00:14:05,018
This is impressive.
Is that a new dock?

292
00:14:05,105 --> 00:14:07,150
No, uh, a pier. Wait.
A wharf.

293
00:14:07,237 --> 00:14:08,935
Would you call
that a wharf?

294
00:14:09,587 --> 00:14:11,676
It's big.

295
00:14:11,763 --> 00:14:14,244
I'm expanding my business.
I need more room.

296
00:14:18,858 --> 00:14:21,295
Look, I got no idea why
some girl wrote

297
00:14:21,382 --> 00:14:23,906
whatever she wrote,
but I never heard of her.

298
00:14:23,993 --> 00:14:26,126
You're barking up
the wrong tree.

299
00:14:26,213 --> 00:14:28,215
Maybe it was somebody
who works for you.

300
00:14:28,302 --> 00:14:31,522
We've been out at sea for the
past week. We just got back.

301
00:14:31,609 --> 00:14:33,873
I told you,
it doesn't make sense.

302
00:14:33,960 --> 00:14:37,311
Even if this girl of yours did
come here, we were all gone.

303
00:14:37,398 --> 00:14:39,443
Your murder's got nothing
to do with us.

304
00:14:43,186 --> 00:14:45,319
It's a pretty airtight alibi.

305
00:14:45,406 --> 00:14:47,408
Yeah. Dock's interesting,
though.

306
00:14:47,495 --> 00:14:49,149
It is?

307
00:14:49,236 --> 00:14:51,716
Well, the Pacific Ocean's
been horribly overfished.

308
00:14:51,803 --> 00:14:54,589
No fisherman these days needs
to build a larger dock.

309
00:14:54,676 --> 00:14:57,244
You think that has something
to do with the murder?

310
00:14:57,331 --> 00:14:59,159
Yes. No.

311
00:14:59,246 --> 00:15:01,030
Not necessarily, no. Maybe.

312
00:15:01,117 --> 00:15:02,423
I just think
it's interesting.

313
00:15:02,510 --> 00:15:04,904
Or not. Let's go talk
to the rancher.

314
00:15:08,255 --> 00:15:10,170
[Lisbon]
Whoever put Talia Suarez
in that cove

315
00:15:10,257 --> 00:15:12,389
had to have carried her
across your property.

316
00:15:12,476 --> 00:15:15,740
[Jed]
'My ranch is 300 acres, and
Wrackers cove's on the far end.'

317
00:15:15,827 --> 00:15:19,092
You could carry a passel load of
bodies without anybody knowing.

318
00:15:19,179 --> 00:15:22,008
What brought you to the island?
Who are you running from?

319
00:15:22,095 --> 00:15:23,748
- What?
- Mm?

320
00:15:23,835 --> 00:15:25,402
I wasn't running
from anybody.

321
00:15:25,489 --> 00:15:28,231
Only I look around this room,
I see horses and cows

322
00:15:28,318 --> 00:15:30,581
but I don't see
any friends or family.

323
00:15:30,668 --> 00:15:32,888
No kids. No sweethearts.

324
00:15:32,975 --> 00:15:34,455
[sighs]
I like horses.

325
00:15:34,542 --> 00:15:38,198
Ah. You're a homely
but passionate man.

326
00:15:38,285 --> 00:15:40,243
So my guess is,
your heart was broken

327
00:15:40,330 --> 00:15:45,161
by a beautiful but cruel
and faithless woman.

328
00:15:45,248 --> 00:15:47,163
30 years ago.

329
00:15:47,250 --> 00:15:49,687
What the hell's that got
to do with this girl?

330
00:15:49,774 --> 00:15:51,559
Probably nothing.
What's your position

331
00:15:51,646 --> 00:15:53,865
on the increased ferry service?

332
00:15:53,953 --> 00:15:55,476
[chuckles]

333
00:15:55,563 --> 00:15:57,608
You're pokin'
at me again, huh?

334
00:15:58,696 --> 00:16:00,176
I'll tell you my position.

335
00:16:00,263 --> 00:16:02,439
Of course, we need
more ferry service.

336
00:16:02,526 --> 00:16:05,051
These islanders got
no business sense at all.

337
00:16:05,138 --> 00:16:06,704
Not even Jack Lafleur?

338
00:16:06,791 --> 00:16:09,533
He struck me as someone that
knows how to do business.

339
00:16:09,620 --> 00:16:11,492
[chuckles]
Oh, he did, did he?

340
00:16:13,102 --> 00:16:14,843
Mr. Stack.

341
00:16:14,930 --> 00:16:18,455
Don't mind my asking, but where
were you the night before last?

342
00:16:18,542 --> 00:16:20,327
Just like always,
right here.

343
00:16:20,414 --> 00:16:22,111
Is there anyone
who can verify that?

344
00:16:22,198 --> 00:16:23,591
Sure. My ranch hands.

345
00:16:23,678 --> 00:16:25,114
Can we talk to them?

346
00:16:25,201 --> 00:16:27,247
Sure, you can.
They're in the corral.

347
00:16:27,334 --> 00:16:29,553
Jane, we'd better--

348
00:16:32,252 --> 00:16:34,994
Why don't you lead the way,
Mr. Stack?

349
00:16:35,081 --> 00:16:36,125
[sighs]

350
00:16:46,701 --> 00:16:49,051
Watch he doesn't kick.

351
00:16:50,096 --> 00:16:51,793
What happened to you?

352
00:16:51,880 --> 00:16:52,881
Horse did.

353
00:16:52,968 --> 00:16:54,709
Oh.

354
00:16:54,796 --> 00:16:57,407
We were culling steer.
Had about 40 head of cattle.

355
00:16:57,494 --> 00:16:59,018
39 head.

356
00:16:59,105 --> 00:17:01,107
- I said "about."
- No such thing as "about."

357
00:17:01,194 --> 00:17:03,718
Either you got 40 head,
or you got 39 head.

358
00:17:03,805 --> 00:17:06,808
Whit would correct the good lord
if he got the count wrong.

359
00:17:06,895 --> 00:17:09,115
We were taking 'em up
to the south pasture.

360
00:17:09,202 --> 00:17:12,161
One got spooked, went over
the side, and took me with it.

361
00:17:12,248 --> 00:17:14,250
I don't know what made
Whit holler more.

362
00:17:14,337 --> 00:17:17,558
A horse rolling over him or
going to the mainland to set it.

363
00:17:17,645 --> 00:17:19,516
- Shut up.
- You don't like the mainland?

364
00:17:19,603 --> 00:17:21,301
Or you don't like
the ferryboat ride?

365
00:17:21,388 --> 00:17:22,911
Neither. I like it here.

366
00:17:22,998 --> 00:17:24,173
Why?

367
00:17:24,260 --> 00:17:25,261
It's quiet.

368
00:17:25,348 --> 00:17:27,046
Yeah.

369
00:17:27,133 --> 00:17:29,700
Seems, uh, old man Stack
feels the same way, huh?

370
00:17:29,787 --> 00:17:33,791
Listen, between you and me...

371
00:17:33,878 --> 00:17:36,620
this whole cowboy thing is
an act with Stack, isn't it?

372
00:17:36,707 --> 00:17:38,144
I mean, he's all hat

373
00:17:38,231 --> 00:17:40,059
and, uh, no stirrups,
as you say?

374
00:17:40,146 --> 00:17:42,191
It's all hat
and no cattle.

375
00:17:42,278 --> 00:17:44,280
- What-what is it?
- All hat and no cattle.

376
00:17:44,367 --> 00:17:46,326
That's what you call
a man like that.

377
00:17:46,413 --> 00:17:47,892
[Jed]
'A man like what?'

378
00:17:47,979 --> 00:17:50,852
Oh, I was just asking about
old, uh, cowboy sayings.

379
00:17:50,939 --> 00:17:53,333
Whit was just correcting him.
Weren't you, whit?

380
00:17:53,420 --> 00:17:55,378
If you'll excuse me,
I gotta take care

381
00:17:55,465 --> 00:17:57,641
of something in the barn.

382
00:17:57,728 --> 00:18:00,166
Sure. Thanks, whit.

383
00:18:03,212 --> 00:18:06,128
Fella's a bit shy,
but, uh, he's a good worker.

384
00:18:06,215 --> 00:18:08,174
- I'd like to interview him.
- No need, Lisbon.

385
00:18:08,261 --> 00:18:10,741
I've already spoken to, uh,
Mr. Stack's workforce here.

386
00:18:10,828 --> 00:18:12,526
We should leave. Thank you.

387
00:18:12,613 --> 00:18:15,529
Where were the three of you
the night before last?

388
00:18:15,616 --> 00:18:17,618
Here. Like always.

389
00:18:17,705 --> 00:18:20,621
Okay. We can go now.
Thank you for your time.

390
00:18:20,708 --> 00:18:22,231
We'll be in touch.

391
00:18:22,318 --> 00:18:24,103
[cell phone rings]

392
00:18:25,539 --> 00:18:26,627
Lisbon.

393
00:18:26,714 --> 00:18:28,455
We got a break
in the case.

394
00:18:28,542 --> 00:18:31,284
A series of incoming calls
to Talia Suarez's motel.

395
00:18:31,371 --> 00:18:34,330
All from a cell phone registered
to a guy named Dennis Kagan.

396
00:18:34,417 --> 00:18:35,505
Who's Dennis Kagan?

397
00:18:35,592 --> 00:18:36,811
'An ex-foster kid who shared'

398
00:18:36,898 --> 00:18:38,639
a couple of group homes
with Talia.

399
00:18:38,726 --> 00:18:41,381
The last call came within a
10-mile radius of Talia's motel.

400
00:18:41,468 --> 00:18:42,686
'Sounds interesting.'

401
00:18:42,773 --> 00:18:44,645
Yeah, especially since
Kagan lives

402
00:18:44,732 --> 00:18:46,212
100 miles Southwest
of the motel.

403
00:18:46,299 --> 00:18:47,822
Are we bringing him in?

404
00:18:47,909 --> 00:18:50,564
Van Pelt and Cho are gonna
pick him up right now.

405
00:18:53,784 --> 00:18:56,744
This is the address.

406
00:18:56,831 --> 00:18:58,485
Apartment's
on the second floor.

407
00:18:58,572 --> 00:19:01,923
- You get a picture of Kagan?
- Yeah, from the DMV.

408
00:19:02,010 --> 00:19:03,142
Here.

409
00:19:05,927 --> 00:19:06,971
'That's him.'

410
00:19:07,058 --> 00:19:08,016
Yeah.

411
00:19:08,103 --> 00:19:09,626
Okay. Let's go.

412
00:19:13,587 --> 00:19:14,631
I'll take lead.

413
00:19:14,718 --> 00:19:17,243
Got it.

414
00:19:17,330 --> 00:19:19,897
Hey. Dennis, right?
You Dennis Kagan?

415
00:19:21,812 --> 00:19:23,205
Maybe.

416
00:19:23,292 --> 00:19:24,946
You're friends
with Talia Suarez, right?

417
00:19:25,033 --> 00:19:26,991
I don't know the name, man.
Keep walkin'.

418
00:19:27,078 --> 00:19:29,516
- I think you do know.
- Hey, get off me, bitch.

419
00:19:29,603 --> 00:19:31,561
Stay right there.

420
00:19:31,648 --> 00:19:33,389
- You're under arrest.
- Uhh!

421
00:19:33,476 --> 00:19:34,912
Put your hands
behind your back...

422
00:19:34,999 --> 00:19:36,523
Get off.

423
00:19:36,610 --> 00:19:39,090
O-okay. I'll take it from here.
Come on. Come on.

424
00:19:52,756 --> 00:19:55,716
[sighs]

425
00:19:55,803 --> 00:19:59,937
It'd be better for you
if you talked to me, Dennis.

426
00:20:00,024 --> 00:20:01,983
You're already looking
at assaulting a police officer.

427
00:20:02,070 --> 00:20:03,811
Don't make it harder
on yourself.

428
00:20:03,898 --> 00:20:06,683
Look, I told you
I want a lawyer.

429
00:20:06,770 --> 00:20:09,164
One's on the way from
the Public Defender's office.

430
00:20:09,251 --> 00:20:11,949
I just wanna give you
a chance to work with us.

431
00:20:12,036 --> 00:20:13,908
Earn yourself
some goodwill.

432
00:20:16,519 --> 00:20:19,261
Hey, it's up to you
how you want to play it.

433
00:20:19,348 --> 00:20:21,350
A lawyer, that's how.

434
00:20:22,656 --> 00:20:24,571
Alright.

435
00:20:24,658 --> 00:20:26,529
Have it your way.

436
00:20:28,879 --> 00:20:29,924
[elevator bell dings]

437
00:20:31,665 --> 00:20:33,841
Hi. You must be from
the public defenders office.

438
00:20:33,928 --> 00:20:35,321
I'm agent Grace Van Pelt.

439
00:20:35,408 --> 00:20:38,149
Grace. Oh. Hi. Right.
Nice to meet you.

440
00:20:38,237 --> 00:20:39,455
I'm Sarah Harrigan.

441
00:20:39,542 --> 00:20:42,502
- Do we know each other?
- No. Um, I j--

442
00:20:42,589 --> 00:20:45,069
Last year there was
a murder at a dating service

443
00:20:45,156 --> 00:20:46,854
and you guys
interviewed me.

444
00:20:46,941 --> 00:20:48,377
Don't worry.
I didn't do it.

445
00:20:48,464 --> 00:20:50,336
[laughing]

446
00:20:52,207 --> 00:20:54,427
Hey, Cho. This is Sarah Harrigan

447
00:20:54,514 --> 00:20:55,776
from the Public Defender's
office.

448
00:20:55,863 --> 00:20:57,212
- Hey.
- Hello, Agent..

449
00:20:57,299 --> 00:20:58,866
Hey, guys. I can handle this.

450
00:20:58,953 --> 00:21:01,564
Sarah, let me bring you
to your client.

451
00:21:05,089 --> 00:21:07,527
- Did that feel weird to you?
- Yeah, a little bit.

452
00:21:07,614 --> 00:21:10,486
Really? What was that all about?

453
00:21:10,573 --> 00:21:12,749
Well, it seems obvious.
They're dating.

454
00:21:18,581 --> 00:21:20,844
So, Dennis,
you've got your lawyer.

455
00:21:20,931 --> 00:21:24,152
- Let's talk.
- I've spoken with my client.

456
00:21:24,239 --> 00:21:26,894
He's willing to help you,
with some stipulations.

457
00:21:26,981 --> 00:21:29,070
We're not making any deals.

458
00:21:31,551 --> 00:21:32,682
[Sarah]
'Go ahead.'

459
00:21:32,769 --> 00:21:35,424
Look, Talia was looking
for the guy

460
00:21:35,511 --> 00:21:37,339
that killed her dad.

461
00:21:37,426 --> 00:21:39,385
'Cause the damn cops
didn't do their job.

462
00:21:39,472 --> 00:21:41,648
My client is speaking
from a place of emotion.

463
00:21:41,735 --> 00:21:43,302
- Means no disrespect.
- They didn't.

464
00:21:43,389 --> 00:21:44,868
They knew who did the murder

465
00:21:44,955 --> 00:21:46,870
and they never bothered
to find him.

466
00:21:46,957 --> 00:21:50,265
And this suspect..

467
00:21:50,352 --> 00:21:51,788
You ever hear his name?

468
00:21:51,875 --> 00:21:53,224
Uh..

469
00:21:53,312 --> 00:21:55,009
...I'm trying to remember.

470
00:21:55,096 --> 00:21:56,750
[sighing]
A funny name

471
00:21:56,837 --> 00:21:59,230
like, uh, Tommy Tuna
or something like that.

472
00:21:59,318 --> 00:22:01,320
[sighing]
Fish?

473
00:22:01,407 --> 00:22:03,496
Yeah. That's the guy.

474
00:22:03,583 --> 00:22:07,500
Uh, Edward Fish.
It was Edward Fish. Yeah.

475
00:22:09,110 --> 00:22:11,808
So you went to her motel.
Why?

476
00:22:11,895 --> 00:22:13,462
[sighing]

477
00:22:13,549 --> 00:22:15,986
'Cause I wanted
to stop her, you know?

478
00:22:16,073 --> 00:22:18,032
Look, what Talia was doing
was dangerous, okay?

479
00:22:18,119 --> 00:22:21,122
She liked to act tough,
but she wasn't, really.

480
00:22:22,471 --> 00:22:25,344
She had my back
in the homes.

481
00:22:27,694 --> 00:22:30,261
I should have had hers..

482
00:22:32,742 --> 00:22:34,701
...but I was too late.

483
00:22:40,576 --> 00:22:42,404
There you are.

484
00:22:42,491 --> 00:22:44,406
Oh, thank you very much.

485
00:22:44,493 --> 00:22:46,234
That looks delicious.
Did you make that?

486
00:22:46,321 --> 00:22:48,279
Mm-hmm.

487
00:22:48,367 --> 00:22:51,892
What do you know about this,
uh, rancher, Stack?

488
00:22:51,979 --> 00:22:54,416
They keep to themselves,
mostly.

489
00:22:54,503 --> 00:22:56,375
He seems like a polite man.

490
00:22:58,594 --> 00:23:01,249
That's delicious.

491
00:23:01,336 --> 00:23:03,599
I thought about
what you said this morning.

492
00:23:08,343 --> 00:23:11,477
You made me feel rather guilty
about that poor girl.

493
00:23:11,564 --> 00:23:14,044
Oh. I'm-I'm sorry. I don't want
anyone to feel guilty

494
00:23:14,131 --> 00:23:15,872
unless, of course,
they are.

495
00:23:15,959 --> 00:23:17,265
Are you?

496
00:23:17,352 --> 00:23:18,571
[chuckling]
Oh, no.

497
00:23:18,658 --> 00:23:21,095
No. I thought not.

498
00:23:21,182 --> 00:23:24,490
I threw a flower
into the ocean for her.

499
00:23:24,577 --> 00:23:25,839
You did?

500
00:23:25,926 --> 00:23:28,624
It's an old island custom.

501
00:23:28,711 --> 00:23:30,409
'My mother said
it sent a message'

502
00:23:30,496 --> 00:23:33,020
to your people
who had passed over.

503
00:23:33,107 --> 00:23:35,501
I mean, I know
the girl isn't my family

504
00:23:35,588 --> 00:23:39,026
but I think it'll
still work, don't you?

505
00:23:39,113 --> 00:23:41,811
Well, it can't hurt.

506
00:23:41,898 --> 00:23:43,683
[chuckling]

507
00:23:43,770 --> 00:23:46,642
You think
I'm a foolish old woman

508
00:23:46,729 --> 00:23:47,948
but I'll tell you this.

509
00:23:48,035 --> 00:23:49,166
[cellphone ringing]

510
00:23:49,253 --> 00:23:50,994
I feel better.

511
00:23:51,081 --> 00:23:52,561
[ringing continues]

512
00:23:52,648 --> 00:23:54,433
I feel like I did..

513
00:23:54,520 --> 00:23:56,783
...something to give
that girl some peace.

514
00:23:56,870 --> 00:23:58,741
[ringing continues]

515
00:24:01,004 --> 00:24:03,398
[ringing continues]

516
00:24:03,485 --> 00:24:06,314
Lisbon, you're calling
to tell me that

517
00:24:06,401 --> 00:24:08,142
the, uh, person of interest
you're following

518
00:24:08,229 --> 00:24:09,578
is not the murderer.

519
00:24:09,665 --> 00:24:12,363
No, but he told us
what Talia was looking for.

520
00:24:12,451 --> 00:24:13,930
Oh?

521
00:24:14,017 --> 00:24:17,151
She was looking for her father's
murderer, Edward Fish.

522
00:24:17,238 --> 00:24:18,587
Hmm.

523
00:24:18,674 --> 00:24:20,197
'Yeah, you'd better
get back here.'

524
00:24:20,284 --> 00:24:22,025
Excuse me, Agent Lisbon.
A word?

525
00:24:22,112 --> 00:24:24,071
- Uh..
- Hmm.

526
00:24:24,158 --> 00:24:26,377
You sound busy, Lisbon,
so don't worry about me.

527
00:24:26,465 --> 00:24:29,032
I know exactly what to do.
Bye.

528
00:24:29,119 --> 00:24:30,120
[sighing]

529
00:24:30,207 --> 00:24:31,295
[receiver clattering]

530
00:24:31,382 --> 00:24:32,558
Yes, counselor?

531
00:24:32,645 --> 00:24:35,212
Yeah,
my client Dennis Kagan..

532
00:24:35,299 --> 00:24:37,171
You are dropping
all charges, I assume

533
00:24:37,258 --> 00:24:39,086
since he's been
such a cooperative witness?

534
00:24:39,173 --> 00:24:41,349
And I've got an agent
with cuts and bruises

535
00:24:41,436 --> 00:24:43,046
as a result
of your client.

536
00:24:43,133 --> 00:24:44,874
And I've got assault

537
00:24:44,961 --> 00:24:46,963
failure to identify,
and excessive force.

538
00:24:47,050 --> 00:24:48,791
Do we really want
to go there

539
00:24:48,878 --> 00:24:50,576
given where your guys
have been already?

540
00:24:50,663 --> 00:24:55,058
It'll never hold up in court,
and you know it, but okay.

541
00:24:55,145 --> 00:24:56,364
Oh, fantastic.

542
00:24:56,451 --> 00:24:59,062
You're meaner
than you look.

543
00:24:59,149 --> 00:25:00,586
Yeah, you are.

544
00:25:00,673 --> 00:25:02,979
[chuckling]
Oh, I try.

545
00:25:06,243 --> 00:25:09,029
- We're still on for tonight?
- Oh, yeah. Sure. Okay.

546
00:25:11,858 --> 00:25:15,383
I'm gonna research
the dad's murder.

547
00:25:15,470 --> 00:25:16,645
Good idea.

548
00:25:20,040 --> 00:25:23,783
[telephone rings,
police radio chatter]

549
00:25:23,870 --> 00:25:25,915
[ring]

550
00:25:26,002 --> 00:25:27,613
hey, boss.

551
00:25:27,700 --> 00:25:30,703
I just made a new pot.
You want a cup?

552
00:25:30,790 --> 00:25:32,052
No.

553
00:25:32,139 --> 00:25:33,662
It's funny
you should mention coffee

554
00:25:33,749 --> 00:25:36,186
because I got off the phone
with a social worker

555
00:25:36,273 --> 00:25:39,538
who claims one of my agents
threw a cup of it at her.

556
00:25:39,625 --> 00:25:42,062
What? It spilled on her desk
as we were leaving.

557
00:25:42,149 --> 00:25:43,629
Didn't even get on her.

558
00:25:43,716 --> 00:25:44,934
Sure you don't want any?

559
00:25:45,021 --> 00:25:46,283
Tell me what's going on

560
00:25:46,370 --> 00:25:48,329
whether or not I can
count on you.

561
00:25:48,416 --> 00:25:51,245
Of course you can.
Why would you even ask me that?

562
00:25:51,332 --> 00:25:53,595
Because right before I spoke
to the social worker

563
00:25:53,682 --> 00:25:55,379
a lawyer from
the public defenders office

564
00:25:55,466 --> 00:25:58,339
threatened to bring you up
on charges of excessive force.

565
00:25:58,426 --> 00:26:00,646
- I'd to cut her client loose.
- You did what?

566
00:26:00,733 --> 00:26:03,692
I'm the one with the busted lip.
He didn't have a mark.

567
00:26:03,779 --> 00:26:05,651
You didn't touch him?

568
00:26:08,001 --> 00:26:10,612
Boss, come on.

569
00:26:10,699 --> 00:26:13,223
So you're telling me that
even though I've gotten

570
00:26:13,310 --> 00:26:15,748
two complaints about you
within the space of an hour

571
00:26:15,835 --> 00:26:17,706
that there's nothing
going on?

572
00:26:17,793 --> 00:26:18,968
You're fine?

573
00:26:19,055 --> 00:26:21,188
Yeah, that's what
I'm saying.

574
00:26:21,275 --> 00:26:24,800
Okay. Look, I know
it's been a tough year.

575
00:26:24,887 --> 00:26:28,369
We've got a new boss.
We're under a lot of scrutiny.

576
00:26:28,456 --> 00:26:32,025
It's really important that
we make a good impression.

577
00:26:32,112 --> 00:26:35,245
- Do you understand?
- I didn't do anything wrong.

578
00:26:35,332 --> 00:26:37,378
Grace.

579
00:26:37,465 --> 00:26:40,294
As a friend, should I be
worried about you?

580
00:26:40,381 --> 00:26:41,600
I'm fine.

581
00:26:41,687 --> 00:26:43,123
The social worker's
just pissed off

582
00:26:43,210 --> 00:26:45,647
that somebody was trying
to make her do her job

583
00:26:45,734 --> 00:26:47,431
and the kid got
out of line.

584
00:26:47,518 --> 00:26:48,781
It's not a big deal.

585
00:26:48,868 --> 00:26:52,219
- The lawyer'd have had no case.
- Alright.

586
00:26:52,306 --> 00:26:54,264
Well, as your boss,
any more of this

587
00:26:54,351 --> 00:26:56,440
and we've got
a serious problem.

588
00:26:56,527 --> 00:26:58,747
Got it.

589
00:26:58,834 --> 00:27:01,141
[police radio chatter]

590
00:27:01,228 --> 00:27:04,100
[birds chirping]

591
00:27:20,464 --> 00:27:22,379
Hi.

592
00:27:22,466 --> 00:27:24,904
Hello.

593
00:27:24,991 --> 00:27:26,862
Mr. Jane, right?

594
00:27:26,949 --> 00:27:28,864
That's right.

595
00:27:28,951 --> 00:27:31,780
Wow. This place is amazing.
Did you do this all yourself?

596
00:27:34,304 --> 00:27:37,307
No. The monarchs do
most of it.

597
00:27:37,394 --> 00:27:39,875
I just try to make their journey
a little easier.

598
00:27:39,962 --> 00:27:43,444
And that's, uh, because insects
deserve a better life?

599
00:27:43,531 --> 00:27:46,621
I know. It's a foolish
obsession, isn't it?

600
00:27:46,708 --> 00:27:49,406
I often wonder..

601
00:27:49,493 --> 00:27:52,061
...why I do it,
but here I am.

602
00:27:52,148 --> 00:27:54,194
Here you are.

603
00:27:54,281 --> 00:27:57,284
But if the cruise ships make
this island a port of call

604
00:27:57,371 --> 00:27:59,329
'this could be a wonderful
tourist attraction.'

605
00:27:59,416 --> 00:28:01,505
You could make yourself
a lot of money.

606
00:28:01,592 --> 00:28:03,507
Cruise ships?
What are you talking about?

607
00:28:03,594 --> 00:28:05,379
Oh, Jack Lafleur..
Do you know him?

608
00:28:05,466 --> 00:28:07,163
The fisherman, yeah.

609
00:28:07,250 --> 00:28:10,906
Yeah, well, apparently
he's, uh, rebuilding his jetty

610
00:28:10,993 --> 00:28:13,822
into, uh, a pier or a wharf,
whichever's bigger.

611
00:28:16,085 --> 00:28:18,566
Why would he do that?
Where'd he get the money?

612
00:28:18,653 --> 00:28:20,002
Well,
I'm not entirely sure

613
00:28:20,089 --> 00:28:22,309
but, uh, I think the money's
coming from stack.

614
00:28:22,396 --> 00:28:25,616
I think the idea is to lure
cruise ships, uh, to the island.

615
00:28:25,704 --> 00:28:27,531
Oh, my god.

616
00:28:27,618 --> 00:28:30,230
I have to make some calls
to the coastal commission.

617
00:28:30,317 --> 00:28:32,536
Um, there's gotta be a way
to stop this.

618
00:28:32,623 --> 00:28:35,496
I suggest the, uh,
Sierra Club, too.

619
00:28:38,064 --> 00:28:40,153
[knock on door]

620
00:28:40,240 --> 00:28:42,851
What's up?

621
00:28:42,938 --> 00:28:44,548
Winston Suarez..
Talia's dad.

622
00:28:44,635 --> 00:28:46,812
Worked as a night guard
in a construction business

623
00:28:46,899 --> 00:28:48,596
near Modesto

624
00:28:48,683 --> 00:28:51,686
and this is the guy
who killed him..

625
00:28:51,773 --> 00:28:55,298
...Edward William Fish,
A.K.A. Eddy Fish.

626
00:28:55,385 --> 00:28:56,909
Nothing to do
with Jack Lafleur.

627
00:28:56,996 --> 00:28:59,041
So Talia went to go
and find Fish

628
00:28:59,128 --> 00:29:00,869
and he killed her
when she did?

629
00:29:00,956 --> 00:29:04,220
Uh, what about the rape?
Does that fit in with his MO?

630
00:29:04,307 --> 00:29:06,701
Fish was a crackhead who stabbed
Winston Suarez 18 times

631
00:29:06,788 --> 00:29:08,268
when he tried to stop Fish

632
00:29:08,355 --> 00:29:09,617
from robbing
the construction company.

633
00:29:09,704 --> 00:29:11,097
That's a lot of blood.

634
00:29:11,184 --> 00:29:12,838
Fish disappeared
with over 300 grand.

635
00:29:12,925 --> 00:29:15,362
Cops never found him.
Assumption is he used the money

636
00:29:15,449 --> 00:29:16,885
to change his appearance
and identity.

637
00:29:16,972 --> 00:29:18,452
What's the connection
to San Felix?

638
00:29:18,539 --> 00:29:20,280
Talia found
the case file.

639
00:29:20,367 --> 00:29:23,196
San Felix's mentioned
as an early lead.

640
00:29:23,283 --> 00:29:25,067
Alright. Thanks.

641
00:29:27,548 --> 00:29:29,463
- Jane.
- 'Hey, Lisbon.'

642
00:29:29,550 --> 00:29:32,161
I'm just putting some of the
finishing touches in place.

643
00:29:32,248 --> 00:29:33,467
'You be careful.'

644
00:29:33,554 --> 00:29:35,512
This Eddy Fish
is a dangerous man.

645
00:29:35,599 --> 00:29:37,776
He stabbed Talia's father
18 times.

646
00:29:37,863 --> 00:29:40,039
It's a good thing
we're about to catch him.

647
00:29:40,126 --> 00:29:42,476
Let's not get ahead
of ourselves.

648
00:29:42,563 --> 00:29:45,131
[chuckling]
If he's on this island, Lisbon

649
00:29:45,218 --> 00:29:47,133
he will reveal
himself to us.

650
00:29:47,220 --> 00:29:48,699
Hang on.

651
00:29:48,787 --> 00:29:50,789
Interesting, huh, guys?

652
00:29:50,876 --> 00:29:53,269
Oh, did...did you hear
about what's happening

653
00:29:53,356 --> 00:29:54,749
with Stack and Gardiner?

654
00:29:54,836 --> 00:29:56,098
What?

655
00:29:56,185 --> 00:29:57,665
Stack has been working
with Lafleur

656
00:29:57,752 --> 00:30:00,102
to try to bring cruise ships
into the harbor.

657
00:30:00,189 --> 00:30:01,800
- Cruise ships?
- Here?

658
00:30:01,887 --> 00:30:03,323
Yeah, and then Gardiner
found out

659
00:30:03,410 --> 00:30:05,194
and he's vowed to stop it
immediately.

660
00:30:05,281 --> 00:30:06,761
What?

661
00:30:06,848 --> 00:30:09,111
We've got to call
a town meeting right away.

662
00:30:11,200 --> 00:30:13,637
Better hurry, Lisbon.
You'll miss all the fun.

663
00:30:13,724 --> 00:30:15,596
I've practically got
the killer in handcuffs.

664
00:30:25,171 --> 00:30:28,348
[indistinct conversation]

665
00:30:28,435 --> 00:30:31,133
[Jack]
What business does anybody
have to do with my dock?

666
00:30:31,220 --> 00:30:33,179
Well, now as a community,
it's everyone's concern..

667
00:30:33,266 --> 00:30:35,442
I-I know this island's
a jewel in the rough.

668
00:30:35,529 --> 00:30:37,226
I'm just lookin'
to polish it up.

669
00:30:37,313 --> 00:30:39,141
No, listen,
the environmental danger

670
00:30:39,228 --> 00:30:41,883
of ferry service pales in
comparison to cruise ships!

671
00:30:41,970 --> 00:30:45,147
The chair has not recognized
Mr. Gardiner.

672
00:30:45,234 --> 00:30:48,107
[Patrick]
Ladies and gentlemen,
sorry for the interruption.

673
00:30:48,194 --> 00:30:49,630
'This is a top priority.'

674
00:30:49,717 --> 00:30:51,850
I'm here with the CBI.
It's okay.

675
00:30:51,937 --> 00:30:54,330
Everything is completely
under control.

676
00:30:54,417 --> 00:30:58,160
I'm gonna need you to follow
my instructions to the letter.

677
00:30:58,247 --> 00:31:02,034
Listen to everything I say
very carefully.

678
00:31:02,121 --> 00:31:06,734
The first thing I need you to do
is to place your coffee cups

679
00:31:06,821 --> 00:31:09,084
on those two tables
back there.

680
00:31:09,171 --> 00:31:11,826
All of you, your coffee cups
on the table back there.

681
00:31:11,913 --> 00:31:13,480
[singsong voice]
For this to work

682
00:31:13,567 --> 00:31:16,004
you're gonna have to listen
and do as you're told.

683
00:31:16,091 --> 00:31:17,788
[normal voice]
Thank you.

684
00:31:17,876 --> 00:31:20,791
Your coffee cups on the table.
This is a serious matter.

685
00:31:20,879 --> 00:31:22,141
[indistinct conversation]

686
00:31:22,228 --> 00:31:23,751
'You too, please.
Thank you.'

687
00:31:23,838 --> 00:31:26,058
'And then return
to your seats..'

688
00:31:26,145 --> 00:31:29,844
...as quickly
and efficiently..

689
00:31:29,931 --> 00:31:32,238
...as you can.

690
00:31:34,327 --> 00:31:36,024
'Thank you.'

691
00:31:37,112 --> 00:31:39,027
Okay. That wasn't so bad.

692
00:31:39,114 --> 00:31:41,769
We've tracked the killer
to this island.

693
00:31:41,856 --> 00:31:42,988
[woman #1]
'The killer's here?'

694
00:31:43,075 --> 00:31:44,380
Yes.

695
00:31:44,467 --> 00:31:47,340
He's here in this room
right now.

696
00:31:51,692 --> 00:31:54,913
His name is Eddy Fish.

697
00:31:55,000 --> 00:31:56,175
[crowd murmuring]

698
00:31:56,262 --> 00:31:59,352
I don't know which one
of you that is

699
00:31:59,439 --> 00:32:02,485
but you killed
Talia Suarez's father.

700
00:32:02,572 --> 00:32:04,923
That's why she came here..
To find you.

701
00:32:07,577 --> 00:32:09,362
Now I'm here..

702
00:32:09,449 --> 00:32:12,539
'...to finish off
what Talia started.'

703
00:32:12,626 --> 00:32:14,541
'Yes.'

704
00:32:14,628 --> 00:32:16,369
[clearing throat]

705
00:32:19,720 --> 00:32:21,504
To catch Eddy Fish.

706
00:32:27,989 --> 00:32:31,688
- What are you going to do?
- Me?

707
00:32:31,775 --> 00:32:33,429
Nothing.
It's already done.

708
00:32:33,516 --> 00:32:36,258
The fingerprints on the cups..

709
00:32:36,345 --> 00:32:37,738
[man #1]
What? No way.

710
00:32:37,825 --> 00:32:39,740
...match them
to the original crime scene.

711
00:32:39,827 --> 00:32:41,698
[indistinct conversation]

712
00:32:46,355 --> 00:32:49,271
- Is that legal?
- N-no, I want my cup back.

713
00:32:49,358 --> 00:32:51,534
Don't worry. I'm gonna get
a camera to document

714
00:32:51,621 --> 00:32:54,189
everything they're doing.
We have rights.

715
00:32:54,276 --> 00:32:56,713
I don't know why you're
all getting so worked up.

716
00:32:56,800 --> 00:32:58,367
Only one of you is guilty.

717
00:32:58,454 --> 00:33:00,674
The rest of you should
just sit back, relax

718
00:33:00,761 --> 00:33:02,632
'have another piece of cake.'

719
00:33:07,637 --> 00:33:09,813
Am I the only one thinking
that's the last

720
00:33:09,900 --> 00:33:11,815
we'll see of Mr. Gardiner?

721
00:33:23,610 --> 00:33:26,004
[starting motor]

722
00:33:44,152 --> 00:33:46,024
You going somewhere,
Mr. Gardiner?

723
00:33:48,678 --> 00:33:50,332
[grunting]
Aah!

724
00:33:50,419 --> 00:33:52,508
No! Let go of me!
I'll kill you!

725
00:33:52,595 --> 00:33:54,075
[grunting]

726
00:33:54,162 --> 00:33:56,121
Eddy Fish, you're under
arrest for the murders

727
00:33:56,208 --> 00:33:57,687
of Winston Suarez
and Talia Suarez.

728
00:33:57,774 --> 00:33:58,993
[grunting and panting]

729
00:33:59,080 --> 00:34:00,603
You've the right
to remain silent.

730
00:34:00,690 --> 00:34:03,954
Anything you say can be used
against you in a court of law.

731
00:34:04,042 --> 00:34:06,000
You've the right
to speak to an attorney.

732
00:34:06,087 --> 00:34:07,523
If you cannot afford
an attorney

733
00:34:07,610 --> 00:34:09,047
one will be appointed
to you.

734
00:34:09,134 --> 00:34:10,874
I didn't know who she was.

735
00:34:10,961 --> 00:34:13,703
I didn't kill her!
I never even saw the girl.

736
00:34:13,790 --> 00:34:15,749
I didn't know
she was on the island.

737
00:34:15,836 --> 00:34:17,707
[indistinct conversation]

738
00:34:22,190 --> 00:34:23,496
Who'd have thought?

739
00:34:23,583 --> 00:34:26,020
He always seemed like
such a nice man.

740
00:34:26,107 --> 00:34:28,849
Mm. He did, didn't he?

741
00:34:35,508 --> 00:34:37,423
Boat's heading over
to transport us back now.

742
00:34:37,510 --> 00:34:39,468
I'm gonna find someplace
to hold the prisoner.

743
00:34:39,555 --> 00:34:41,253
Good idea.
Tell Cho and Van Pelt.

744
00:34:41,340 --> 00:34:42,993
Okay.

745
00:34:43,081 --> 00:34:45,779
Well, we do thank you, ma'am.

746
00:34:45,866 --> 00:34:48,303
That was some nice work
you all did.

747
00:34:48,390 --> 00:34:50,436
Thank you, Mr. Stack.
Just doing our job.

748
00:34:50,523 --> 00:34:53,917
I did wonder about the man.
It was a feeling I had.

749
00:34:54,004 --> 00:34:55,702
I don't know
what it was exactly

750
00:34:55,789 --> 00:34:57,704
but, uh, normal people
don't feed crickets.

751
00:34:57,791 --> 00:34:59,053
Butterflies.

752
00:34:59,140 --> 00:35:01,534
Whatever. Bugs.

753
00:35:01,621 --> 00:35:04,537
The point is,
I got instincts about people.

754
00:35:04,624 --> 00:35:08,193
[Patrick laughing]
No, you don't.

755
00:35:08,280 --> 00:35:10,020
You were fooled,
just like everybody else.

756
00:35:10,108 --> 00:35:11,326
You callin' me a liar?

757
00:35:11,413 --> 00:35:13,372
No, it's only that
now you know him

758
00:35:13,459 --> 00:35:15,852
as Eddy Fish,
the violent sociopath.

759
00:35:15,939 --> 00:35:18,420
It's easy for you
to see him as a killer.

760
00:35:18,507 --> 00:35:21,162
But as the shy lepidopterist,
Gardiner?

761
00:35:21,249 --> 00:35:24,557
Impossible for anyone to imagine
him stabbing a man 14 times.

762
00:35:24,644 --> 00:35:27,908
- Eighteen times.
- Right. 18. 18 times.

763
00:35:27,995 --> 00:35:32,652
Unimaginable, and a number
that you can't forget

764
00:35:32,739 --> 00:35:34,044
once you've heard it.

765
00:35:34,132 --> 00:35:35,959
'Eighteen times.'

766
00:35:36,046 --> 00:35:37,961
Where did you hear it
exactly?

767
00:35:38,048 --> 00:35:41,835
What? Oh, uh, from you,
uh, earlier.

768
00:35:41,922 --> 00:35:45,012
No, not from me.
Where then?

769
00:35:45,099 --> 00:35:48,102
I-I don't...I don't know
what you're talking about.

770
00:35:48,189 --> 00:35:50,365
Gardiner killed
Talia's father.

771
00:35:50,452 --> 00:35:53,542
That's true, but, uh,
he didn't kill Talia.

772
00:35:53,629 --> 00:35:55,065
Well, he might have

773
00:35:55,153 --> 00:35:57,329
'if he'd known she was here
looking for him'

774
00:35:57,416 --> 00:35:58,852
'but he wouldn't have
raped her.'

775
00:35:58,939 --> 00:36:01,507
No, not his thing.

776
00:36:01,594 --> 00:36:03,248
You did it.

777
00:36:03,335 --> 00:36:06,076
'You met her on the ferryboat
coming back from the hospital.'

778
00:36:06,164 --> 00:36:07,948
[chuckling]
What? No.

779
00:36:08,035 --> 00:36:10,777
Wow. You say no,
but your eyes tell me yes.

780
00:36:10,864 --> 00:36:13,127
[Terence]
Talia asked for your help.

781
00:36:13,214 --> 00:36:15,477
She told you about
her father and Fish

782
00:36:15,564 --> 00:36:17,087
and the 18 stab wounds.

783
00:36:17,175 --> 00:36:19,612
That's how you got that
scratch on your face.

784
00:36:19,699 --> 00:36:22,049
You're strong enough to hold
a girl down single-handed

785
00:36:22,136 --> 00:36:23,659
but you couldn't protect
your face.

786
00:36:23,746 --> 00:36:25,618
Gardiner killed her.
We all saw.

787
00:36:25,705 --> 00:36:26,923
No.

788
00:36:27,010 --> 00:36:28,186
You're wrong.

789
00:36:28,273 --> 00:36:30,623
Doubtful.

790
00:36:30,710 --> 00:36:32,233
Proving it should
be easy, though

791
00:36:32,320 --> 00:36:34,714
since you gave us
your DNA to match.

792
00:36:36,629 --> 00:36:38,935
Cuff him, Lisbon.

793
00:36:41,503 --> 00:36:43,375
[motor rumbling]

794
00:36:45,159 --> 00:36:46,987
Does it make you
uncomfortable, Mr. Naylor

795
00:36:47,074 --> 00:36:48,423
being alone here with me?

796
00:36:48,510 --> 00:36:50,556
It should.

797
00:36:50,643 --> 00:36:53,298
I'd gladly hurt you for
what did to that poor girl

798
00:36:53,385 --> 00:36:54,734
hurt you badly.

799
00:36:54,821 --> 00:36:57,040
Whoa, agent.
Cool it down, okay?

800
00:36:57,127 --> 00:36:58,564
It's not his fault
what happened.

801
00:36:58,651 --> 00:37:00,479
Oh, please.
It's not his fault?

802
00:37:00,566 --> 00:37:01,784
No.

803
00:37:03,656 --> 00:37:05,135
You're a decent guy.

804
00:37:05,223 --> 00:37:06,920
I can tell.

805
00:37:07,007 --> 00:37:08,487
If you could have
controlled yourself

806
00:37:08,574 --> 00:37:10,315
you would have, right?

807
00:37:10,402 --> 00:37:12,534
You don't want to hurt anyone.

808
00:37:14,449 --> 00:37:15,972
- No.
- No.

809
00:37:16,059 --> 00:37:18,888
When you get those urges,
it's-it's like somebody else

810
00:37:18,975 --> 00:37:21,413
taking over your body, right?

811
00:37:21,500 --> 00:37:22,892
Somebody evil?

812
00:37:26,113 --> 00:37:27,462
Yeah.

813
00:37:27,549 --> 00:37:28,768
[scoffing]
Come on.

814
00:37:28,855 --> 00:37:30,378
No, really.

815
00:37:36,602 --> 00:37:38,821
[box scraping floor]

816
00:37:38,908 --> 00:37:40,649
Listen..

817
00:37:42,782 --> 00:37:45,654
...I understand that you were
trying to control yourself.

818
00:37:45,741 --> 00:37:48,178
That's why you went
to Stack's ranch on San Felix..

819
00:37:48,266 --> 00:37:50,964
...to take yourself away
somewhere safe

820
00:37:51,051 --> 00:37:53,053
somewhere away
from temptation, right?

821
00:37:54,533 --> 00:37:56,056
But then you hurt
your shoulder

822
00:37:56,143 --> 00:37:57,840
and a doctor needed
to set it

823
00:37:57,927 --> 00:38:00,321
and there's no doctor
on the island.

824
00:38:00,408 --> 00:38:03,237
I wanted to stay
on the ranch.

825
00:38:03,324 --> 00:38:05,544
I didn't..

826
00:38:05,631 --> 00:38:07,110
I didn't want to leave.

827
00:38:07,197 --> 00:38:09,765
And she was on the ferry
coming back, wasn't she?

828
00:38:09,852 --> 00:38:11,724
She asked about your arm.

829
00:38:12,986 --> 00:38:14,857
She came to me.

830
00:38:17,033 --> 00:38:19,732
She asked for my help

831
00:38:19,819 --> 00:38:21,255
wanted me to hide her.

832
00:38:21,342 --> 00:38:23,257
Yeah, so you took her
to the ranch.

833
00:38:23,344 --> 00:38:26,869
An outbuilding..

834
00:38:26,956 --> 00:38:28,480
...where nobody goes.

835
00:38:28,567 --> 00:38:31,396
Where no one would hear
when you raped and killed her.

836
00:38:33,528 --> 00:38:35,965
You can't judge me.

837
00:38:36,052 --> 00:38:38,272
You don't know
what it's like to be me.

838
00:38:38,359 --> 00:38:40,318
You think I want to be
like this?

839
00:38:40,405 --> 00:38:43,364
You have the gall to ask
for pity after what you've done?

840
00:38:43,451 --> 00:38:45,888
You'll get no pity

841
00:38:45,975 --> 00:38:47,803
not in this life
or the next.

842
00:38:55,681 --> 00:38:57,378
[sighing]

843
00:39:06,953 --> 00:39:09,651
Nice work back there.

844
00:39:09,738 --> 00:39:11,653
Thanks. You too.

845
00:39:11,740 --> 00:39:13,960
Yeah, we really had him
fooled, huh?

846
00:39:14,047 --> 00:39:16,005
He thought you were
a real hard case.

847
00:39:16,092 --> 00:39:17,920
[chuckling]
Yeah.

848
00:39:18,007 --> 00:39:19,966
Listen, about before
at the office with Sarah

849
00:39:20,053 --> 00:39:21,489
I just want to..

850
00:39:21,576 --> 00:39:23,752
Oh, yeah. Congratulations.
She seems nice.

851
00:39:23,839 --> 00:39:26,320
Yeah, I just don't want it
to be awkward between..

852
00:39:26,407 --> 00:39:27,626
[laughing]
With me?

853
00:39:27,713 --> 00:39:29,279
That's the least
of your problems.

854
00:39:29,367 --> 00:39:30,890
What does that mean?

855
00:39:30,977 --> 00:39:33,240
Well,
she's a public defender.

856
00:39:33,327 --> 00:39:36,025
I mean, good luck,
but be careful.

857
00:39:36,112 --> 00:39:38,767
[Lisbon]
'Hey. The boat's docking now.'

858
00:39:38,854 --> 00:39:41,117
Get the prisoners
and get 'em on board.

859
00:39:41,204 --> 00:39:43,424
- Yeah. Sure thing, boss.
- Have you seen Jane?

860
00:39:43,511 --> 00:39:45,121
- No.
- No.

861
00:39:45,208 --> 00:39:47,602
Better get here quick,
or he's gonna get left behind.

862
00:39:51,650 --> 00:39:53,216
[sighing]

863
00:40:46,835 --> 00:40:49,055
[theme music]

